/**
\page org_mitk_views_interactivefiberdissection atTRACTive

Interactively train a classifier for tract dissection. The workflow implemented in atTRACTive has been published in [1].

\section SecDissectionInput Input Data

- Selected Fiber Bundle: Currently selected fiber bundle (can be necessary for operations not belonging to ATTRACTIVE).
- Training Fiber Bundle: Fibers belonging to that bundle are chosen to be annotated, and the model predicts on all streamlines from that bundle. It is possible to switch the bundle during the iterations, e.g., train first with Tractogram 1 and once the prediction is accurate, switch to Tractogram 2 and predict on its data. The prototypes (see "Number of prototypes") stay those of the first tractogram after the switch, so the features of both tractograms are comparable; the annotations of both are used for training.

\section SecDissectionPreprocessing Preprocessing

- Resample Bundle: Resamples every streamline of the training bundle to 40 points, equally spaced along the streamline with both end points kept, as a new bundle. Necessary to calculate dissimilarity features proposed by Olivetti et al. [2]. Needs to be done prior to training.
- Number of prototypes: Define the number of prototypes and hence, the number of features to be generated per streamline. More prototypes mean more features and eventually increase the performance of the classifier. However, more features also mean more computational effort. We found 75 to be a good tradeoff since during the segmentation process iterative features are calculated (refer to our publication [1]).

\section SecDissectionActiveLearning Active Learning
Iteratively use active learning to extract the individual chosen target tract by annotating streamlines that the classifier proposes to the human expert by its own uncertainty.

1. Initialization:

From Region-based Tractography:
If the tractogram contains a good balance of fibers belonging to the target tract and fibers not belonging to it, you can simply click the "Add" button. A subset of randomly selected fibers will be presented for annotation. Swipe over the fibers and press Alt if a fiber belongs to the target tract, or Shift if it doesn't. Annotated fibers are visible in the Datamanager (+Bundle for fibers belonging to the target tract and -Bundle for fibers not belonging to it). Annotations can be corrected by swiping the cursor over the fibers and pressing Ctrl. The fiber under the cursor is highlighted in orange. The brush button switches the annotation off and on again while the fibers are presented; "Add" switches it on.

From Whole Brain Tractography:
Since the number of fibers in specific white matter tracts might only be a fraction of the whole tractogram's fibers, randomly sampling might not catch enough or even any target fibers. To improve this, draw two ROIs (Regions of Interest). Create the start ROI by using the mouse middle button to define a sphere's center. The sphere's radius is set by cursor movement, and clicking the middle button creates the start ROI. Repeat for the end ROI, generating a "Reduced" bundle with the streamlines that end in both spheres, in either direction. Half of the randomly sampled streamlines are then drawn from this bundle. Annotation proceeds as before by clicking the "Add" button.

Once annotation is complete, click "Train classifier." Features are calculated, and a random forest is trained. The classifier predicts all streamlines of the selected tractogram that are not annotated, providing a "Prediction<n>" bundle (n counts the training cycles from 0) that also contains the streamlines annotated as belonging to the tract.

2. Uncertainty-based Annotation:
If the classifier's prediction is not accurate, more fibers need to be annotated by the human expert. Simply click the "Add" button, and new fibers are presented for annotation. These fibers are selected depending on the uncertainty and dissimilarity. Repeat until the prediction of the classifier is satisfactory. If a small number of false positive streamlines are presented, you can exclude these fibers manually.

3. Outlier Exclusion:
If the prediction is almost correct, but some single outliers exist, it is possible to exclude those with the labeling brush of the prediction: Shift removes a fiber from the prediction without adding it to -Bundle, Alt moves it to +Bundle. Afterwards, it is possible to save the bundle and continue with the next subject.

Reset Classifier:
To delete all annotated fibers and start dissection from scratch, it is possible to reset the classifier. Resetting stops the annotation tools; the last prediction stays in the Data Manager. However, it is possible to use the ability of the classifier to generalize and to use the already annotated fibers, so the already achieved knowledge, to segment the same white matter tract for a different subject.

Enable/Disable Workflow Help Messages:
If the workflow is novel for you, help messages are presented before each action. After some time, they become obsolete, so feel free to disable.

\section SecDissectionTwoSpheres Dissecting a Tract with Two Spheres

atTRACTive is the recommended way to obtain a single tract, in this view as for the Workbench agent (\ref SecDissectionRest): two rough regions and a few rounds of labeling give the tract with its fanning and crossing parts, which ROIs alone lose or mix with neighbouring bundles. Extraction by ROIs (the manual page "Streamline Tractography", section "ROI Strategy") stays the fallback.

1. Two spheres at the tract's two ends, as two labels of one segmentation: one sphere where the tract's fibers end on each side. The tracking does not stop in the spheres; it keeps a fiber only if the fiber's two ends lie in them, and a fiber ends where the model's values fall below the cutoff (usually where the white matter meets the grey matter of the cortex) or where it leaves the mask or the image. So each sphere covers the tract's whole start or end region: all the cortex where its fibers end, or the border of the image or the brain mask where the tract leaves it (the corticospinal tract in the brain stem). The size of the spheres therefore depends on the tract; for the arcuate fasciculus, one sphere covers the posterior inferior frontal and the ventral precentral gyrus and one the posterior superior, middle and inferior temporal gyri, each about 30 mm in radius. A sphere may reach beyond its region into white matter or cerebrospinal fluid: make it rather too large than too small, since the labeling removes the fibers of other tracts later, but cannot add fibers that the tracking did not produce. On an HCP subject, arcuate spheres of 15 mm radius kept 495 fibers (0.017 per seed), of 25 mm 11,337 fibers (0.10 per seed), and spheres of 30 mm over the whole end regions 10,751 fibers from one seed per voxel (0.12 per seed). Keep the two spheres apart: a voxel in both belongs to one of the two labels only, and a fiber that ends there is lost. A sphere on the tract's course keeps only the few fibers that happen to stop in it: on the same subject, spheres in the cerebral peduncle and under the precentral gyrus kept 62 corticospinal fibers of 41,710 seeds. The recipes of the manual page "Streamline Tractography", section "Recipes for Common Tracts", name the end regions of common tracts.
2. Tractography between the spheres: seeds in both spheres, and a fiber is kept only if its two ends lie in the two different spheres. In the Streamline Tractography view: the segmentation with both labels as seed image and as target image, endpoint constraint "Both EPs are required to be located in the target image and the image values at the respective position needs to be distinct". Through the REST API: seed and target {segmentation, labels: [a, b]} and endpoint_constraint "both_in_target_different_labels". Track on peaks (the manual page "ODF Peak Extraction"), probabilistically so that the fanning parts are covered, with a brain mask and 1 to 5 seeds per voxel. Tracking on the SH image itself is not suited here: on the HCP subject, its loop check (loop_check_deg 30) ended most fibers after about 26 mm, and without the loop check the fibers grew implausibly long (median 190 mm) and the tracking took more than ten times as long as on peaks. Some hundred to a few thousand fibers are enough. With few fibers, look at their lengths in the metrics: fibers as long as the tract with few of them mean that the spheres miss most fiber ends (enlarge them so that they cover the whole end regions, or move them); short fibers mean that the tracking stops early (track on peaks, lower the cutoff). If the spheres cannot be placed at the ends, track the whole brain and give the spheres to the session instead (below), or dissect the tract by ROIs (the fallback).
3. A session on the result: an initial sample, labels, a training, and then uncertain samples and further trainings until the prediction stops changing (\ref SecDissectionStop).

The fibers between the spheres all connect the two regions, so the regions alone do not separate the tract from fibers that take a wrong route between them (through the other hemisphere, the cerebellum or a neighbouring bundle); the labels teach the classifier which routes belong to the tract.

A session also works on a whole-brain tractogram. The two spheres then select the fibers with one end in each, in either direction, and half of every initial sample is drawn from them (in this view the start and end ROI, through the REST API the spheres of the session). A tractogram of more than 80,000 fibers is reduced in the first training to the 20 % of its fibers closest to the positive labels, so label fibers of the tract positive in the first cycle.

\section SecDissectionDescriptors Labeling by Descriptors

Without a mouse, a fiber is judged from its description. The REST API describes every sampled fiber by its id, its length in millimeters, its two ends and five points from end to end at equal distances along it (world coordinates in millimeters, as the sphere centres), and, with a landmark segmentation, the names of the labels it ends in at each end and of all labels it touches. A screenshot of the node "ToLabel" shows the sample as a whole; the descriptions judge each fiber.

- A fiber of the tract has one end in each end region of the tract, runs the tract's course between them (compare the five points with it: the corticospinal tract runs superior-inferior through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the arcuate fasciculus arches around the Sylvian fissure), and has about the tract's length.
- Landmark labels make the judgement explicit. Draw them as labels of a segmentation (spheres or polygons, POST /api/v1/diffusion/rois): at the tract's stem, a region every fiber of the tract passes (the posterior limb of the internal capsule for the corticospinal tract), and where wrong fibers go (the midsagittal plane, the middle cerebellar peduncle, the corpus callosum, a neighbouring bundle). A fiber that ends in both end regions and passes the stem is positive; a fiber that touches the landmark of a wrong route is negative.
- Label clear cases only and leave doubtful fibers unlabeled: a wrong label moves the classifier's boundary, an unlabeled fiber only comes back in a later sample. Label fibers of both classes; the first training needs at least one positive and one negative label.
- After a training, a description says whether the classifier puts the fiber into the tract. A fiber of the prediction that does not belong to the tract is labeled negative and leaves the tract at the next training; a missed fiber of the tract is labeled positive.

\section SecDissectionStop When to Stop

Every training reports changed_fraction: the fibers that entered or left the prediction since the last training, relative to the fibers of either prediction (1 after the first training). Stop when changed_fraction stays small, below about 0.05 (5 %), over two trainings and a screenshot of the prediction shows no fibers outside the tract; then save the prediction as the tract. As long as it changes more, sample the most uncertain fibers, label them and train again; three to six cycles of 10 to 20 fibers are typical. A falling number of uncertain fibers (classified with an entropy of at least 0.95) is a further sign that the classifier has settled. The value of 0.05 and the two quiet trainings are provisional; trials on real data will adjust them.

\section SecDissectionRest REST Endpoints

The REST API of the Workbench (the REST API view; the Agent view works through it) offers the sessions of this view below /api/v1/diffusion/attractive, labeled by fiber id instead of the brush. A session of the API is independent of this view: its nodes ("ToLabel", "+Bundle", "-Bundle", "Prediction<n>") appear below the tractogram in the Data Manager, but the view does not continue it. GET /api/v1/endpoints?path=/api/v1/diffusion/attractive&details=true describes every parameter.

- POST /api/v1/diffusion/attractive/sessions: starts a session on a tractogram as a job (resampling, prototypes), with an optional seed (the same samples and predictions at every run), the two spheres for a whole-brain tractogram, and a landmark segmentation.
- GET /api/v1/diffusion/attractive/sessions and GET .../sessions/{id}: the sessions, and the state of one: labels, trainings, the numbers of the last training, its nodes.
- POST .../sessions/{id}/sample: fibers to label, "initial", "uncertain" or "diverse", with their descriptions (\ref SecDissectionDescriptors), shown as "ToLabel".
- POST .../sessions/{id}/labels: positive, negative and unlabel by fiber id, shown as "+Bundle" and "-Bundle".
- POST .../sessions/{id}/train: trains the classifier and predicts the tract as a job; adds "Prediction<n>" and reports changed_fraction (\ref SecDissectionStop).
- POST .../sessions/{id}/finish: the prediction as a tract with the tractogram's own fibers at full resolution.
- DELETE .../sessions/{id}: ends the session and removes "ToLabel", "+Bundle" and "-Bundle"; predictions and tracts stay.

\section SecDissectionReferences References

[1] Peretzke, R. (2023). atTRACTive: Semi-automatic white matter tract segmentation using active learning. Proceedings of the 26th International Conference on Medical Image Computing and Computer-Assisted Intervention (MICCAI 2023) (pp. 123-135). Springer.
[2] Olivetti, E., Avesani, P.: Supervised segmentation of fiber tracts. In: International Workshop on Similarity-Based Pattern Recognition. pp. 261–274. Springer (2011)

*/
